    "body": "Finding the right fit: AI-generated image\nBy Alex Lennane\n18 November 2026\nWhen WiseTech overhauled CargoWise pricing last December, some forwarders told The Loadstar they had few realistic alternatives. That appears to be slowly changing.\nNow, for example, FreightSuite and Shipthis are pitching themselves as CargoWise replacements, GoFreight is courting similar customers, and Magaya has named two forwarders that chose its software over CargoWise.\nMagaya even points to the moves on its website, saying: “CargoWise, built by WiseTech Global, has long dominated the highest enterprise tier of freight forwarding software. Magaya was built for a different kind of company: small to mid-market freight forwarders and customs brokers who run integrated warehouse operations, value responsive support, and need software they can actually go live on without a six-month project and a dedicated consultant.\n“That distinction has always existed, but recent events at WiseTech Global have brought it into sharp focus, prompting forwarders across the industry to take a hard look at what they’re paying, what they’re getting, and what alternatives look like.”\nBut how far do those alternatives extend for forwarders that need one system across multiple countries?\nThat question has reached the European Commission. In August, a submission seen by The Loadstar asked the EC to monitor whether CargoWise’s reach and WiseTech’s acquisitions could make it harder for forwarders to switch, or for rival vendors to connect to customs systems on equal terms. The submission alleged no breach of competition law. The commission said it had put the information on file.\nIn Australia, the competition regulator took action in a separate case. The ACCC accepted a court-enforceable undertaking from WiseTech and its subsidiary, BluJay, to divest Expedient, a logistics software business acquired through e2open. The regulator said WiseTech already had substantial market power and that bringing CargoWise and Expedient under the same ownership reduced choice for Australian customers. WiseTech and BluJay disputed that assessment.\nRivals acknowledge how hard it is to compete for the largest accounts. “There is really no alternative to CargoWise for the biggest global forwarders,” Benjamin Riege of Germany’s Riege Software told The Loadstar in February. “But when you’re a medium-sized forwarder, of course we are a choice.”\nFor a multinational, switching means getting the new software to work with the customs authorities, carriers, and customers its branches already deal with. Can staff file declarations from the new platform in every country in which they need to? Can the finance teams keep invoicing while open jobs and unbilled charges are moved across? Will staff still be able to retrieve the records they need for an audit?\nAI may help vendors build customs integrations faster, one freight software executive told The Loadstar , but “the actual government sign-off is always going to be the bottleneck”. The same executive sees a reason for AI vendors to take on the core platform itself. “The system of record is going to be what owns the AI and the process, going forward,” he said.\nRivals can point to some wins. Magaya said in January that EP Logistics had selected its software to replace CargoWise across its US and Mexican operations. In May, it announced that Montreal-based CMS Shipping had also chosen to switch. WiseTech, meanwhile, said it had secured CargoWise rollouts with 13 of the world’s 25 largest forwarders as of 30 June, and named Blue Water Shipping and XPD Global among the contracts won under its new pricing model.\nThe next CargoWise renewal may be where forwarders first feel the benefit of more competition. A credible alternative gives them more room to negotiate on price and support, even if they ultimately decide to stay.",
